WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.710
Hostname authorization and address
authorization answer different questions.

2
00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:09.305
A hostname is an application-level name;
DNS determines candidate network addresses.

3
00:00:09.385 --> 00:00:13.655
One name may resolve to several IPv4 and
IPv6 results, and those results can change.

4
00:00:13.735 --> 00:00:16.420
For a feature intended to reach the public
Internet,

5
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:20.893
every address the connection may select
must satisfy the public-destination policy

6
00:00:20.973 --> 00:00:22.681
before any connection is opened.

7
00:00:22.761 --> 00:00:26.727
Checking only the first answer leaves
unchecked candidates available to the

8
00:00:26.807 --> 00:00:27.417
transport.

9
00:00:27.497 --> 00:00:31.035
The policy should classify parsed
addresses, not textual prefixes.

10
00:00:31.115 --> 00:00:34.837
At minimum, an Internet-fetching service
normally excludes loopback,

11
00:00:34.917 --> 00:00:37.663
private-use, link-local, multicast,
unspecified,

12
00:00:37.743 --> 00:00:40.915
and other special-purpose or
deployment-forbidden ranges.

13
00:00:40.995 --> 00:00:45.327
Production policy may also exclude
organization-owned networks that are globally

14
00:00:45.407 --> 00:00:46.993
numbered but still sensitive.

15
00:00:47.073 --> 00:00:50.795
Use a well-tested IP parser and range
classifier informed by the current

16
00:00:50.875 --> 00:00:52.888
special-purpose address registries.

17
00:00:52.968 --> 00:00:57.178
Account for both address families and
equivalent forms such as IPv4-mapped IPv6.

18
00:00:57.258 --> 00:01:00.553
Hand-maintained string tests are too easy
to make incomplete or

19
00:01:00.633 --> 00:01:02.158
representation-dependent.

20
00:01:02.238 --> 00:01:05.411
IP literals need the same classification
without a DNS query.

21
00:01:05.491 --> 00:01:09.639
Hostnames should be resolved through the
name-resolution path that matches the

22
00:01:09.719 --> 00:01:11.550
eventual connection environment.

23
00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:16.145
In Node, operating-system resolution and
direct DNS queries are not interchangeable:

24
00:01:16.225 --> 00:01:18.116
they can consult different sources.

25
00:01:18.196 --> 00:01:20.331
Whichever mechanism the design selects,

26
00:01:20.411 --> 00:01:24.743
request all applicable results and reject
the destination if any candidate that the

27
00:01:24.823 --> 00:01:26.592
client could use violates policy.

28
00:01:26.672 --> 00:01:31.126
Treat no result, resolution errors, and
malformed answers as denial, not permission.

29
00:01:31.206 --> 00:01:35.477
A business allowlist is stronger and
simpler when the service talks to a small known

30
00:01:35.557 --> 00:01:39.584
set of providers, but a trusted name does
not automatically make an unexpected

31
00:01:39.664 --> 00:01:40.396
address safe.

32
00:01:40.476 --> 00:01:44.564
Monitor approved names and validate their
resolved addresses according to the

33
00:01:44.644 --> 00:01:46.657
deployment's intended network model.

34
00:01:46.737 --> 00:01:51.008
For a broad public-URL feature,
classification is an explicit security boundary.

35
00:01:51.088 --> 00:01:54.748
The decision must cover the address the
transport can actually choose,

36
00:01:54.828 --> 00:01:57.940
not merely the hostname that appeared in
the original input.
